WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:01.449
What for?

00:00:01.449 --> 00:00:05.330
For fun. I'll do anything for fun.

00:00:05.330 --> 00:00:10.679
How much can a single expression convey? Steven
Yeun is one of my favorite actors working

00:00:10.679 --> 00:00:15.280
right now and one of my favorite performances
from him is his restrained ambiguous work

00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:18.670
as Ben in Lee Chong Dang's Burning.

00:00:18.670 --> 00:00:24.570
Jung-Su. Do you like pasta?

00:00:24.570 --> 00:00:31.369
I'm not going to spoil the movies I talk about
in this video but here's some context for

00:00:31.369 --> 00:00:36.790
this one: Jung-Su has randomly reconnected
with a friend from school, Hae-mi who he has

00:00:36.790 --> 00:00:42.250
an unexpressed crush on, meanwhile Hae-mi
while she's off on a trip abroad becomes friends

00:00:42.250 --> 00:00:47.720
with wealthy and mysterious Ben who she's
interested in. It's classic Love Triangle

00:00:47.720 --> 00:00:53.219
stuff but there's another layer here. There's
this scene where they all go to dinner with

00:00:53.219 --> 00:00:58.910
some of Ben's rich friends and Hae-mi at Ben's
encouragement is demonstrating a dance she

00:00:58.910 --> 00:01:03.480
learned while she was traveling Jong-Su is
watching her and then looks over at Ben and

00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:11.090
catches him yawning and then Ben gives him
this look. There's so much in this brief moment;

00:01:11.090 --> 00:01:17.090
a feeling that he doesn't really care, a kind
of contempt, that she's just a play thing

00:01:17.090 --> 00:01:24.770
to him. But there's also this element of bringing
Jun-Su into that feeling. There's a "you know"

00:01:24.770 --> 00:01:30.760
sort of aspect to the look that feels like
it's trying to make Jung-Su complicit in Ben's

00:01:30.760 --> 00:01:35.829
behavior. Something that is found only in
Steven Yeun's performance, that alludes to

00:01:35.829 --> 00:01:42.439
a whole world of subtext that eventually becomes
very important to the film&nbsp;.

