00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:02.200
When people talk about "best performances"
in film a lot of stuff comes up, and a lot

00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:07.630
of it is this kind of thing. The big evocative
roles, the roles that require the actor to

00:02:07.630 --> 00:02:12.300
transform themselves into a character completely
in some way.

00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:17.300
For the Oscars it's even better if that transformation
requires some kind of challenging element

00:02:17.300 --> 00:02:20.489
like weight loss, prosthetic makeup, or an
accent.

00:02:20.489 --> 00:02:24.800
Like so much of the rest of the Oscars it
can sometimes feel like the awards go to most

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:27.349
acting and not necessarily best acting.

00:02:27.349 --> 00:02:31.900
I think one of the reasons The Joker has become
such an enduring screen character isn't necessarily

00:02:31.900 --> 00:02:37.080
because he's just a more compelling villain,
but because he's become a character that is

00:02:37.080 --> 00:02:42.090
a playground for bold expression that the
regular boundaries of performance may not

00:02:42.090 --> 00:02:43.090
allow.

00:02:43.090 --> 00:02:47.849
And actors enjoy lining up and taking their
swing at the role almost as much as audiences

00:02:47.849 --> 00:02:49.720
enjoy watching those swings.

00:02:49.720 --> 00:02:53.480
And this video is not about how these kinds
of performances are bad.

00:02:53.480 --> 00:02:57.150
I love an... "I've abandoned my boy" ...moment
as much as anybody.

00:02:57.150 --> 00:03:02.950
In fact my favorite actor Tilda Swinton, while
she definitely can play subtle roles, often

00:03:02.950 --> 00:03:03.950
doesn't.

00:03:03.950 --> 00:03:07.489
One of the reasons I love her work is in part
because she's willing to go way overboard

00:03:07.489 --> 00:03:12.920
and transform herself into all manner of absurd
character for even small supporting roles.

00:03:12.920 --> 00:03:15.739
So I'm not here to bash that kind of acting
I think it's great.

00:03:15.739 --> 00:03:19.920
But in all the praise for this kind of showy
and transformational acting I think some of

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:24.190
the best performances or moments of performance
can get overlooked.

00:03:24.190 --> 00:03:28.760
So I want to showcase an aspect of performance
that I think is less recognized but no less

00:03:28.760 --> 00:03:30.439
impressive.

00:03:30.439 --> 00:03:35.000
These moments are not ones where the actors
transform themselves but ones where they seem

00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:40.799
to disappear into a character where you can
no longer see the actor on screen performing

00:03:40.799 --> 00:03:47.290
and instead just see their character feeling
the emotion of the drama.

00:03:47.290 --> 00:03:51.700
Often what sets these kind of performances
apart is the subtlety with which these actors

00:03:51.700 --> 00:03:58.170
can convey deep rich and complex emotion with
their faces and their bodies.

00:03:58.170 --> 00:04:01.700
They've embodied the character to the point
where they communicate what they want to communicate

00:04:01.700 --> 00:04:07.239
through the character not just with line delivery
but with even the tiniest of expressions.

00:04:07.239 --> 00:04:12.329
Sometimes these subtler reactions and expressions
can play a very important role within the

00:04:12.329 --> 00:04:17.450
story itself: besides the Steven Yeun character
in "Burning," Daniel Kaluuya in "Get Out"

00:04:17.450 --> 00:04:19.250
is another great example of this.

00:04:19.250 --> 00:04:25.070
Kaluuya plays the big emotions in this movie
well, but even more importantly his reactions

00:04:25.070 --> 00:04:31.320
to the world around him gives the film subtext
a lot of weight and helps guide the audience

00:04:31.320 --> 00:04:33.770
into the absurdity of the experience.

00:04:33.770 --> 00:04:37.539
"Such a privilege to be able to experience
another person's culture"

00:04:37.539 --> 00:04:42.789
-like the way this look right here telegraphs:
"yeah I know what you *really* mean.

00:04:42.789 --> 00:04:48.380
The exact subtext of a lot of Daniel Kaluuya's
reactions in the first part of this movie

00:04:48.380 --> 00:04:52.880
are just as important as any of the lines
the other characters are saying.

00:04:52.880 --> 00:04:56.980
Another recent performance I think is really
great is Florence Pugh in Midsommar.

00:04:56.980 --> 00:05:02.030
She nails the big outpouring of emotion necessary
for certain scenes but I think almost more

00:05:02.030 --> 00:05:05.660
impressive is her command of a subtler, smaller
moments.

00:05:05.660 --> 00:05:09.320
The film opens with the death of her family
and look at this moment where a friend brings

00:05:09.320 --> 00:05:10.320
it up:

00:05:10.320 --> 00:05:18.169
"also I I never had the chance to tell you
but I was I was so very sorry to hear about

00:05:18.169 --> 00:05:19.919
your loss"

00:05:19.919 --> 00:05:20.919
"oh"

00:05:20.919 --> 00:05:22.380
"what happened I mean"

00:05:22.380 --> 00:05:27.080
-I the way her expression sort of melts into
a subtle grief... It's the kind of thing that

00:05:27.080 --> 00:05:33.569
looks like it could only really be an automatic
reaction to a sudden memory or realization.

00:05:33.569 --> 00:05:37.870
There's a similar moment from a bit part in
The Darjeeling Limited that always gets me:

00:05:37.870 --> 00:05:42.010
The three brothers show up at their father's
mechanic to pick up his car on the way to

00:05:42.010 --> 00:05:47.910
his funera,l and the way the mechanic subtly
responds to finding out the father died:

00:05:47.910 --> 00:05:50.460
"you'll have to wait another week I left a
message"

00:05:50.460 --> 00:05:55.680
"yeah he didn't get the message Mr. Whitman
is dead you understand."

00:05:55.680 --> 00:05:58.620
"Jimmy Whitman?"

00:05:58.620 --> 00:06:04.229
...For some reason to me one of the saddest
things in the film.

00:06:04.229 --> 00:06:09.340
Continuing on this theme of grief Rooney Mara
conveys it powerfully in David Lowry's "A

00:06:09.340 --> 00:06:10.340
Ghost Story."

00:06:10.340 --> 00:06:15.380
Sometimes grief can leave you frozen in shock
and that seems like it should be easy to pull

00:06:15.380 --> 00:06:18.009
off for an actor you just don't move.

00:06:18.009 --> 00:06:24.040
But there's a tension and involuntary element
to what looks like genuine shock that is different

00:06:24.040 --> 00:06:25.880
from just not moving

00:06:25.880 --> 00:06:26.880
and here....

00:06:26.880 --> 00:06:34.890
there's the smallest involuntary twitch that
somehow sells this moment as more authentic.

00:06:34.890 --> 00:06:39.051
One of the reasons I think I'm drawn to these
kind of subtle moments is that I think they

00:06:39.051 --> 00:06:45.250
often convey a kind of complexity of emotion
that isn't necessarily there in bigger outbursts.

00:06:45.250 --> 00:06:48.960
Pulling off the big outbursts in a way that
feels authentic is certainly a challenge,

00:06:48.960 --> 00:06:52.380
but what about the nuance of expressing repressed
emotion?

00:06:52.380 --> 00:06:56.949
What about when you want the audience to perceive
that the character is feeling an emotion and

00:06:56.949 --> 00:07:00.879
also perceive that the character is trying
to hold that emotion back?

00:07:00.879 --> 00:07:05.169
Amy Adams does it here in Spike Jones' "Her"
in this moment where her character wants to

00:07:05.169 --> 00:07:08.910
cry but is also obviously trying not to.

00:07:08.910 --> 00:07:14.020
"Charles and I split up."

00:07:14.020 --> 00:07:15.039
"what?"

00:07:15.039 --> 00:07:17.080
"yeah really"

00:07:17.080 --> 00:07:21.759
Or what about the complexity of multiple emotions
arising at once?

00:07:21.759 --> 00:07:26.639
Oscar Isaac nails this in HBO's remake of
"Scenes From a Marriage." In this scene he

00:07:26.639 --> 00:07:32.530
and his wife, played by Jessica Chastain,
decide to keep an accidental pregnancy.

00:07:32.530 --> 00:07:35.150
"Ava'll be thrilled."

00:07:35.150 --> 00:07:37.759
"And your parents."

00:07:37.759 --> 00:07:40.379
"oh God yeah"

00:07:40.379 --> 00:07:43.050
-And this little moment of expression...

00:07:43.050 --> 00:07:46.539
...is amazing.

00:07:46.539 --> 00:07:52.389
The amount of fear hope and trepidatious joy
that are all somehow fighting to come out

00:07:52.389 --> 00:07:58.220
at once, even while he attempts to hold them
back is incredible to me.

00:07:58.220 --> 00:08:02.849
I find this kind of thing compelling because
in my experience I think this is what a lot

00:08:02.849 --> 00:08:08.599
of real life emotion is actually like. Sure
there are the big yelling and punching the

00:08:08.599 --> 00:08:13.819
wall moments for some people, sometimes, but
I think far more frequently there's emotion

00:08:13.819 --> 00:08:19.470
that people are trying to conceal or that
is a tangled mix of different emotions instead

00:08:19.470 --> 00:08:20.760
of just one big one.

00:08:20.760 --> 00:08:25.009
Riz Ahmed has this great moment in "Sound
of Metal" where he asks his mentor and sobriety

00:08:25.009 --> 00:08:26.230
sponsor for money:

00:08:26.230 --> 00:08:35.690
"I was wondering Joe I don't know if it's
possible, I need money."

00:08:35.690 --> 00:08:45.605
"Just to buy back my RV you know me me and
Lou is all we have."

00:08:45.605 --> 00:08:51.640
...and the way grief and shame seem to bubble
to the surface while he fights it back hits

00:08:51.640 --> 00:08:55.279
me every time watching this.

00:08:55.279 --> 00:09:01.329
People talk a lot about range in acting as
the ability to play different types of characters

00:09:01.329 --> 00:09:05.750
but I think there's also a kind of range in
terms of intensity.

00:09:05.750 --> 00:09:09.700
We all know Joe Pesci for the sinister intensity
that he has in "Goodfellas:"

00:09:09.700 --> 00:09:18.020
"funny how? I mean what's funny about it?"
"he's a big boy he knows what he said. What'd

00:09:18.020 --> 00:09:19.910
you say funny how?"

00:09:19.910 --> 00:09:23.790
This guy is right on the edge. You don't want
to mess with him. He feels like he could snap

00:09:23.790 --> 00:09:26.390
at any moment and he's genuinely terrifying.

00:09:26.390 --> 00:09:31.570
"tell me tell me what's funny"

00:09:31.570 --> 00:09:37.339
So it's impressive to me that in "The Irishman"
his character is no less sinister and threatening.

00:09:37.339 --> 00:09:44.339
"There's been a change instead of uh we're
going up right away we're gonna hang around

00:09:44.339 --> 00:09:46.870
tomorrow morning and then go up drive up."

00:09:46.870 --> 00:09:54.120
"But I told Jimmy that we were gonna be up
in the morning I told Jimmy we were going

00:09:54.120 --> 00:09:57.110
to be up in the morning."

00:09:57.110 --> 00:09:58.140
"I know"

00:09:58.140 --> 00:10:03.310
Even though the intensity of the performance
is dialed way back until it almost disappears.

00:10:03.310 --> 00:10:12.170
"We got to go there I mean I gotta go I got
I told him I'd be there."

00:10:12.170 --> 00:10:15.170
"Frank. We did all we could for the man."

00:10:15.170 --> 00:10:21.030
The fact that he can convey something so similar
through completely different levels of intensity

00:10:21.030 --> 00:10:22.839
is why he's one of the greats.

00:10:22.839 --> 00:10:28.450
When I talk about these kinds of subtle expressions
I think rarely are they a very conscious choice

00:10:28.450 --> 00:10:29.529
by the actor.

00:10:29.529 --> 00:10:34.660
I don't think Rooney Mara is acting that scene
thinking "my neck is going to twitch and that's

00:10:34.660 --> 00:10:36.269
going to make it feel more real."

00:10:36.269 --> 00:10:42.579
Rather this kind of subtlety is the result
of the actors finding a way to actually experience

00:10:42.579 --> 00:10:49.529
or feel the emotions they're trying to convey
and the subtle micro expressions are the result

00:10:49.529 --> 00:10:50.529
of that.

00:10:50.529 --> 00:10:55.220
But one of the reasons I've never talked about
acting despite the fact that I've made over

00:10:55.220 --> 00:11:00.320
100 videos about film and television now is
because the craft is kind of a mystery to

00:11:00.320 --> 00:11:01.320
me.

00:11:01.320 --> 00:11:04.660
When I listen to actors talking about their
craft there's not a consistent approach that

00:11:04.660 --> 00:11:08.600
wins out and seems to be the best and when
actors talk about how they accomplish these

00:11:08.600 --> 00:11:09.680
kinds of performances.

00:11:09.680 --> 00:11:15.920
It's often very esoteric and it's all about
arriving at a point of authenticity and sometimes

00:11:15.920 --> 00:11:18.970
completely differing techniques can get you
there.

00:11:18.970 --> 00:11:23.640
Sometimes they refine things through extensive
rehearsal and sometimes actors find that in

00:11:23.640 --> 00:11:25.589
the spontaneity of a first take.

00:11:25.589 --> 00:11:29.459
It's kind of impossible to reverse engineer
a performance just by looking at the screen

00:11:29.459 --> 00:11:32.960
and trying to figure out what the actor did
to get there.

00:11:32.960 --> 00:11:37.930
And so I think talking about technically what
makes for good acting is a pretty difficult

00:11:37.930 --> 00:11:38.930
feat.

00:11:38.930 --> 00:11:44.550
All I can really do is look at these performances
and assess how authentic or unforced it feels

00:11:44.550 --> 00:11:45.550
to me.

00:11:45.550 --> 00:11:50.480
I think a lot of these techniques are just
finding a way to access genuine emotion and

00:11:50.480 --> 00:11:54.779
then this very realistic expression comes
out of that.

00:11:54.779 --> 00:12:00.320
That said if there are performers out there
who have this kind of conscious control of

00:12:00.320 --> 00:12:06.550
minute micro expressions in a way that feels
involuntary... I mean that's almost more impressive.

00:12:06.550 --> 00:12:10.440
There are so many fantastic examples of this
kind of performance out there and this isn't

00:12:10.440 --> 00:12:15.699
meant to be a definitive list or a list of
the best examples of this these are just specific

00:12:15.699 --> 00:12:20.700
examples that have stuck with me because they
impacted me in a certain way and as I was

00:12:20.700 --> 00:12:26.160
looking through all these examples and trying
to understand what quality it is that allows

00:12:26.160 --> 00:12:31.829
this kind of subtle expression to come through
in these performances I noticed that a lot

00:12:31.829 --> 00:12:36.300
of these performances have a quality that
I would call screen presence I think screen

00:12:36.300 --> 00:12:42.170
presence is an interesting idea and it's one
I wanted to explore a little more thoroughly

00:12:42.170 --> 00:12:48.420
so I made a companion video all about that
that is available exclusively on nebula putting

